      "notes": "All four claims are well-supported by their cited evidence. The stated-tier claims correctly quote the policy text. E18 (Children's Commissioner, 19,000-school survey) is a credible government-adjacent source and entails the measurable claim without overstatement. E2 entails the Ofsted enforcement claim. Tier integrity is sound \u2014 no projections dressed as measurables. Direction (worsens, minor) is coherent: the rationale correctly identifies the liberty effect as a new statutory coercion over personal property for minors during school hours, notes the bounded scope, and does not conflate this with surveillance or speech content. Magnitude (minor) and time_horizon (this-parliament) are appropriately calibrated given the narrow population and pre-existing voluntary norms. No party-blind issues \u2014 the rationale judges the modelled effect, not party identity or motive. No false balance \u2014 the verdict does not claim mixed/uncertain; it reaches a clear minor-worsens conclusion supported by the claims. No lever leakage \u2014 the verdict does not score academic outcomes or wellbeing, only the liberty dimension. One minor observation: the claim about 'presumably secure storage' in claim 3 introduces speculation ('presumably') not entailed by the quote, but the claim is not load-bearing for the verdict direction and does not alter the O10 assessment. Not blocking."
    },
